.TH "TEMPS" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
temps \- cPU, drive and board temperatures, and fan speed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
temps [options] [-- sensors options]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBtemps\fR shows how hot the parts of the machine are, one row per part. The CPU, each NVMe or SATA drive, the graphics card, the Wi\-Fi card and the mainboard each get a row, with the reading and the high mark the driver reports for it. Fans show their speed.
.PP
It reads the kernel's hardware monitor files in \fB/sys/class/hwmon\fR directly, so it needs no extra package, no root and no setup. The \fBsensors\fR command from lm\-sensors reads the same files. \fBtemps\fR groups them by part and leaves out the noise, such as the dozen identical readings a CPU with many cores reports.
.PP
\fBtemps\fR only reads. It never changes a fan speed, a power limit or any other setting.
.SS How it names each row
.PP
The first column says what the part is. The second column names the sensor.
.TP
\fBcpu\fR
Drivers: coretemp, k10temp, zenpower, cpu_thermal. What it measures: The processor. \fBpackage\fR is the whole chip, \fBcores\fR lists each core.
.TP
\fBnvme\fR
Drivers: nvme. What it measures: An NVMe SSD, named after its device, such as \fBnvme0n1\fR.
.TP
\fBdrive\fR
Drivers: drivetemp. What it measures: A SATA drive, named after its device, such as \fBsda\fR. The drivetemp module must be loaded.
.TP
\fBgpu\fR
Drivers: amdgpu, radeon, nouveau, i915, xe. What it measures: The graphics chip.
.TP
\fBwifi\fR
Drivers: iwlwifi, ath, mt7, rtw. What it measures: The Wi\-Fi card.
.TP
\fBboard\fR
Drivers: acpitz, nct, it87, thinkpad, dell_smm, asus. What it measures: Mainboard and firmware sensors.
.TP
\fBfan\fR
Drivers: any driver with a fan input. What it measures: Fan speed in revolutions per minute.
.TP
\fBother\fR
Drivers: anything else. What it measures: Sensors \fBtemps\fR does not know, shown with their driver name.
.PP
On Intel CPUs, \fBPackage id 0\fR becomes \fBpackage\fR. On AMD CPUs, \fBTctl\fR becomes \fBpackage\fR. All \fBCore N\fR readings go on one \fBcores\fR row, so an 8\-core CPU takes one line, not 8.
.PP
An NVMe drive often reports two or three sensors. \fBtemps\fR shows the main one, called Composite, and keeps the rest for \fB\-a\fR.
.SS The high mark and hot
.PP
Next to a reading, \fBhigh 100 C\fR is the temperature the driver says the part should stay under. \fBtemps\fR takes the driver's max value, or its critical value when there is no max. A driver that reports 0 or more than 200 as its limit has not set one, so \fBtemps\fR shows none.
.PP
A reading within 5 C of its high mark gets the word \fBhot\fR. A CPU that reaches its high mark slows itself down to cool off, which is why a hot laptop feels slow. A drive at its high mark slows down too, and wears faster.
.SS No hwmon sensors
.PP
Some machines, such as many ARM boards, have no hwmon sensors but do have thermal zones in \fB/sys/class/thermal\fR. \fBtemps\fR falls back to those, and uses each zone's critical trip point as the high mark.
.PP
Virtual machines usually have neither, because the host keeps the hardware to itself. \fBtemps\fR then says \fBno sensors found\fR and exits 1.
.SS Watching
.PP
\fB\-w\fR refreshes the table every 2 seconds, or every SEC seconds. It adds MIN and MAX columns, so you can start a game, a build or a video call and see how hot things got. In a terminal the table redraws in place, with the reminder \fBCtrl+C stops it.\fR under it. When the output goes to a file, each refresh is appended, with a blank line between them, and the reminder goes to stderr once at the start.
.PP
Ctrl+C stops it and prints one line for each part that got hot, with how often and how hot. When nothing got hot, it names the hottest reading instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
Show every raw sensor, with its hwmon file, driver, label, value and high mark.
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-watch [SEC]\fR
Refresh every SEC seconds, 2 by default, with MIN and MAX columns. SEC can have a decimal point, such as \fB0.5\fR. Ctrl+C stops it.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
One line, the hottest CPU reading, such as \fBcpu 71 C\fR. Without a CPU sensor, the hottest reading of all.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show every step.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
Options after \fB\-\-\fR skip the table and run \fBsensors\fR from lm\-sensors with those options. Use it for what only lm\-sensors does, such as Fahrenheit or its own config file.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
temps -- -f
temps -- -u
temps -- -j
.fi
.RE
.PP
This needs the lm\-sensors package. The table itself does not.
.SH PER\-DISTRO NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAll distros.\fR The table works everywhere the kernel has hwmon drivers, which is every desktop distro.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSATA drive temperatures.\fR These need the \fBdrivetemp\fR kernel module, which most distros build but do not load. Run \fBsudo modprobe drivetemp\fR to try it, and add \fBdrivetemp\fR to a file in \fB/etc/modules\-load.d/\fR to load it at every boot.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMainboard sensors.\fR Chips such as nct6775 and it87 need their module loaded. \fBsudo sensors\-detect\fR from lm\-sensors finds and loads them.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlm\-sensors packages.\fR \fBlm\-sensors\fR on Debian, Ubuntu and Alpine, \fBlm_sensors\fR on Fedora and Arch, \fBsensors\fR on openSUSE.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRaspberry Pi.\fR The CPU reports through a thermal zone called \fBcpu\-thermal\fR.
.SH NEEDS
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing for the table. It reads \fB/sys/class/hwmon\fR and \fB/sys/class/thermal\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsensors\fR, from lm\-sensors, for options after \fB\-\-\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS A laptop
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ temps
board    acpitz       45 C  high 120 C
nvme     nvme0n1      39 C  high 82 C
cpu      package      71 C  high 100 C
cpu      cores        69 C  68 C  high 100 C
fan      fan1         3,810 rpm
.fi
.RE
.PP
The CPU is at 71 C with a limit of 100 C. The fan runs, and nothing is hot.
.SS A CPU near its limit
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ temps
board    acpitz       45 C  high 120 C
nvme     nvme0n1      39 C  high 82 C
cpu      package      97 C  high 100 C  hot
cpu      cores        69 C  68 C  high 100 C
fan      fan1         3,810 rpm
.fi
.RE
.PP
At 97 C the CPU slows itself down. Check that the vents are clear and the fan is not full of dust.
.SS One number for a status bar
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ temps -q
cpu 71 C
.fi
.RE
.SS Every raw sensor
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ temps -a
SENSOR                 DRIVER       LABEL                VALUE  HIGH
hwmon0/temp1_input     acpitz       -                     45 C  120 C
hwmon1/temp1_input     nvme         Composite             39 C  82 C
hwmon1/temp2_input     nvme         Sensor 1              39 C
hwmon2/temp1_input     coretemp     Package id 0          71 C  100 C
hwmon2/temp2_input     coretemp     Core 0                69 C  100 C
hwmon2/temp3_input     coretemp     Core 1                68 C  100 C
hwmon3/fan1_input      thinkpad     -                3,810 rpm
.fi
.RE
.PP
The SENSOR column is the file under \fB/sys/class/hwmon\fR. Read it yourself with \fBcat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon2/temp1_input\fR, which gives millidegrees, 71000 for 71 C.
.SS Watch during a heavy job
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ temps -w 1
                       NOW       MIN       MAX
board acpitz          45 C      45 C      45 C
nvme nvme0n1          39 C      39 C      39 C
cpu package           74 C      71 C      96 C
cpu cores             88 C      69 C      88 C
fan fan1         3,810 rpm 3,810 rpm 3,810 rpm
Ctrl+C stops it.
^C
cpu package went within 5 C of its high mark in 1 of 4 readings, the most was 96 C.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The CPU touched 96 C once and came back down. One short peak is normal under load. Many readings near the limit mean the cooling cannot keep up.
.SS A virtual machine
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ temps
temps: no sensors found. Virtual machines usually have none.
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fB`temps: no sensors found.`\fR On a virtual machine or in a container this is expected. On real hardware, the driver for the sensor chip is not loaded. Run \fBsudo sensors\-detect\fR from lm\-sensors and let it load the modules it finds.
.PP
\fBNo row for a SATA drive.\fR Load the drivetemp module, as in the per\-distro notes above. NVMe drives need nothing extra.
.PP
\fBA reading of 0 C or 127 C that never changes.\fR The sensor is not wired up on this board. It shows in \fB\-a\fR under its driver name. Ignore it, or blacklist the driver if it bothers you.
.PP
\fB`temps: needs sensors.`\fR Options after \fB\-\-\fR need lm\-sensors. The message shows the install command. The table works without it.
.PP
\fBThe fan shows 0 rpm.\fR Many laptops stop the fan when the CPU is cool. Watch with \fB\-w\fR under load and the number should rise.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
It worked. With \fB\-w\fR, stopped with Ctrl+C.
.TP
1
No sensor found.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as a name, or \fB\-w 0\fR.
.TP
3
sensors is missing, for options after \fB\-\-\fR.
.TP
143
\fB\-w\fR was stopped by a TERM signal, such as from \fBkill\fR or \fBtimeout\fR. The summary still prints.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBdisks\fR, \fBmem\fR, \fBsysinfo\fR, \fBsensors(1)\fR, \fBsensors\-detect(8)\fR
